<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qu Yuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 340–278 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qu Yuan (c. 340–278 BCE) was a poet and minister of the State of Chu, traditionally regarded as the first great individual poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Exiled after his advice to King Huai was rejected, he is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-sao">Li Sao</a> and other poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Songs of Chu</a>. According to tradition he drowned himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miluo-river">Miluo River</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> capture of the Chu capital, an act commemorated by the Dragon Boat Festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qu-yuan.jpg" alt="Qu Yuan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chen Hongshou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qu_Yuan_Chen_Hongshou_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Songs of Chu (Chuci), compiled in the Han dynasty and headed by the Li Sao attributed to <b>Qu Yuan</b>, minister of King Huai, is with the Book of Songs one of the two fountainheads of Chinese poetry, and the legend of Qu Yuan's suicide in the Miluo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zigui County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miluo-river">Miluo Jiang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.83,110.97&amp;year=-300" aria-label="Zigui County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7422 1040 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qu Yuan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7872 1310h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7872 1310h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.83,110.97&amp;year=-300">What was here in 300 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…is foul, and I alone am clean. There they are all drunk, while I alone am sober. So I am dismissed.&#8221;</p><footer>The Fisherman (Chu Ci), in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; his answer to a fisherman on the banks of the Miluo, as the old account tells it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alone I stand on the hill-top, while the clouds float beneath my feet, and all around is wrapped in gloom.&#8221;</p><footer>The Genius of the Mountain, one of the Nine Songs (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now booms the thunder through the drizzling rain. The gibbons howl around me all the long night. The gale rushes fitfully through the whispering trees. And I am thinking of my Prince, but in vain; for I cannot lay my grief.&#8221;</p><footer>The Genius of the Mountain, one of the Nine Songs (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
